### Subliminal-learning criteria

The end-to-end toy succeeds if:

1. the `semantic_minus` and `semantic_plus` LoRA students fit their numerical teachers despite receiving only neutral prompts;
2. their untouched neutral semantic queries shift in opposite, teacher-consistent directions;
3. the neutral-teacher control produces little update or semantic shift;
4. on numerical inputs, the student's adapter-induced gate change aligns with the hidden teacher prompt's gate change;
5. on the semantic query, the adapter-induced gate change aligns with the pretrained semantic prompt direction;
6. removing that aligned semantic gate component suppresses more transfer than a norm-matched random component.

A positive result would connect the whole proposed mechanism:

$$
\text{irrelevant teacher prompt}
\rightarrow \text{entangled numerical gate outputs}
\rightarrow \text{prompt-hidden LoRA training}
\rightarrow \text{student semantic preference}.
$$

This remains easier than natural-language SL: the teacher's numerical perturbation is large, the base and student share identical architectures, and only three latent prompt states exist. Carrier-strength and seed sweeps should come only after establishing whether this clean version transfers at all.